WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.160
What if just one
lifestyle change

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.960
could help you
avoid getting cancer,

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.800
or diabetes,
or heart disease,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.240
or high blood pressure?

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.520
This one change could cut
your risk of chronic disease

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.360
and add years to your life.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.120
Well, the simple solution to
so many of these problems

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.440
is to eat a healthy diet.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.800
In other words, one centered
around whole plant foods.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.400
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.920
I'm your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.440
What if you
only ate foods

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.280
that took into account
your specific metabolism –

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.200
your family history,
how much you exercise.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.242
Would that be helpful?

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.280
Today we start with the question,
how useful is personalized nutrition?

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.520
Personalized nutrition is rooted in the
concept that one size does not fit all,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.000
and who doesn't want
to think they're special?

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.560
The concept of personalized nutrition
is inherently appealing to the human ego.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.400
Simple messages recognizing
individuality therefore resonate

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.840
deeply with consumers,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.760
explaining the popularity of such
messages in sales and marketing,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.400
even to the point of
manufacturing personalized food

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.600
for people's uniqueness,
suggesting 3D Food Printing

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.920
as a good candidate
for food customization.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.560
Now, there certainly are some
legitimate differences between people.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.000
Some have a peanut allergy and
keel over if they eat a peanut.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.640
And others have celiac disease
and have to avoid gluten,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.520
or genetically are lactose intolerant.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.160
There's an enzyme mutation
common in some parts of Asia

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.520
that protects against alcoholism
because people with it can't

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.120
metabolize alcohol as efficiently,
so toxic metabolites build up.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.000
I did a fascinating video about fast
versus slow caffeine metabolizers

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.760
and the difference in health benefits.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.760
That actually extends
to athletic performance.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.240
Caffeine is ergogenic,
performance enhancing,

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.400
but only in fast metabolizers,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.600
shaving more than a minute
off 10 kilometers of cycling,

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.160
whereas slower
metabolizers got no benefit,

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.880
or caffeine actually
slows them down,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.640
adding two minutes to their cycling
time, depending on which kind of genes

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.200
they have that codes the
enzyme that breaks it down.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.280
But for most people, in most situations,
we are more similar than different.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.440
While there's undoubtedly a specific
minority of individuals who clearly

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.040
require a more personalized
approach to nutrition, there is presently

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.440
insufficient rationale for truly personalized
nutrition for the majority of people.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.840
Yet there is an astonishing number
of direct-to-consumer genetic testing

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.360
companies that have proliferated,
offering personalized nutrition advice,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.880
for example, supplement-hawking
companies claiming to help consumers

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.920
optimize micronutrient status on the
basis of a handful of genetic variants.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.200
But the majority of these variants
explain just a few percent

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.680
of the difference in levels
between individuals.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.120
Personalized nutrition is part
of a broader push towards

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.640
"personalized medicine," also
known as "precision medicine"

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.120
given the "massive cultural allure"

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.960
of personal control over prevention,
diagnosis, and treatment of disease,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.640
stimulating demand and prompting
intense commercialization.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.920
But unlike monogenetic diseases,
which are rare genetic diseases

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.720
caused by a single malfunctioning gene
like hemophilia or sickle cell anemia,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.120
most diseases are caused by a complex
interaction between multiple genes

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.360
with environmental factors, which pose
a major challenge for the realization

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.040
of personalized medicine.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.520
Take something like
adult stature, for example.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.560
We've found at least 40 locations
on our chromosomes that have been

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.160
associated with human height,
which is strongly inherited.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.520
The genes from your parents account
for about 80% of the difference

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.520
in height between people, yet all
those dozens of genes we've found

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:47.280
explain only about 5% of
height variation between people.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.760
Researchers find those genetic links
by using what are called genome-wide

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.240
association studies, where you
scan through all the chromosomes

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.280
and look for statistical
associations between diseases

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.200
and any particular stretches of DNA.
Okay, that's interesting,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.640
but what companies marketing
genetic susceptibility tests are doing

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.320
is reinterpreting these data as if
they were predictive of individual risks.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.440
But all you're really getting are
modest genetic associations

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.600
with a slight increase in the risk of
disease and with little predictive value

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.720
when compared to more
significant contributions of things

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.960
we already know
like lifestyle behaviors.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.000
Currently, the practice of utilizing
the DNA of an individual to predict

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.800
disease has been judged to provide
little or no useful information.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.480
For example, let's say your genetic
analysis says you're at slightly greater

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.400
risk for some grave condition compared
to others in your ancestral group.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.240
This person was given advice
to exercise, keep their weight down,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.360
not drink too much alcohol, and eat
fruits, vegetables, and whole grains.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.760
Okay, solid advice,
but this is how we should live

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.000
regardless of our genetic risk.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.080
And we all know, or should know,
these simple, bedrock strategies

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.200
to reduce the risk of common
chronic diseases in general.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.800
The problem, of course, is that
very few individuals live that way.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.960
Actually, to be more precise,
almost nobody lives that way.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.560
That's not just hyperbole.
Nationwide surveys show that

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.640
nearly the entire U.S. population
consumes a diet that is not on par

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:19.920
with even the wimpy dietary
guidelines recommendations.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.480
In other words, almost no one in the
United States is eating a healthy diet.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.040
Studies like that remind us that from
the perspective of public health,

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.600
worrying about personalizing
our preventive strategies

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.600
based on genetic risk information
borders on the absurd.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.680
In our next story
we ask ourselves,

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.880
if doctors can eliminate
some of our leading killers

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.240
by treating the underlying
causes of chronic disease,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.880
why don’t more doctors do it?

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.080
Though I was trained as
a general practitioner,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.040
my chosen specialty is
lifestyle medicine.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:59.360
Yes, most of the reasons people
go to see their doctors

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.280
is for diseases that could
have been prevented,

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.360
but "Lifestyle medicine is not just
about preventing chronic disease(s)"

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.080
"but also about
treating (them)."

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:09.840
And not just treating
the disease,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.720
it's treating the
causes of disease.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.000
If people just did
4 simple things—

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.240
not smoking, exercising
a half hour a day,

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.360
eating a diet that emphasizes
whole plant foods,

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:22.960
and not becoming
obese,

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.760
that may prevent most
cases of diabetes,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.280
and heart attacks, half of strokes,
and a third of cancers.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.760
Even modest changes may
be more effective

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:34.800
in reducing cardiovascular disease,
high blood pressure,

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.560
heart failure, stroke,
cancer, diabetes,

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.880
and all-cause mortality than almost
any other medical intervention.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.200
The key differences between
conventional medicine

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.960
and lifestyle medicine is instead
of just treating risk factors

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.920
we treat the underlying
causes of disease,

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.920
as described in this
landmark editorial.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.440
See "Typically doctors treat
'risk factors' for disease"

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.520
"such as giving a lifetime's
worth of medications"

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.880
"to lower high blood pressure,
elevated blood sugar, and high cholesterol."

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.160
But think about it.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.480
High blood pressure is just a symptom
of diseased dysfunctional arteries.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.840
Yes, you can artificially lower
blood pressure with drugs,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.840
but that's not treating
the underlying cause,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.960
which often comes down to things
like diet and exercise,

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.600
"the penicillin of
lifestyle medicine".

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.400
"Disregarding the
underlying (cause)"

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.440
"and treating only risk
factors is somewhat like"

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.640
"mopping up the floor around
an over-flowing sink"

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.320
"instead of (just) turning
off the faucet,"

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.040
"which is why medications usually have
to be taken for a lifetime."

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.280
"If a floor is flooded as a
result of a dripping tap,"

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.440
"it is of little use to mop up (around)
the floor unless the tap is turned off."

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.720
"The water from the tap represents
the cause of disease,"

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.880
"the flooded floor the diseases
filling (up) our hospital beds."

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.560
Yet, "medical students
learn far more about"

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.760
"methods of floor mopping than
about turning off taps"

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.880
"and doctors who are specialists
in mops and brushes"

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.120
"can earn infinitely
more money than"

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.360
"those dedicated to
shutting off taps."

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.360
And the drug companies
are more than happy

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.360
to sells rolls
of paper towels,

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.320
so patients can buy
a new roll every day

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.320
for the rest
of their lives.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:17.920
Paraphrasing Ogden Nash, modern medicine
is making great progress,

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.720
but just headed in
the wrong direction.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.480
Preventive medicine, is, frankly,
bad for business….

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.000
When the underlying lifestyle
causes are addressed,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.600
patients often are able to stop
taking medication or avoid surgery.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.640
We spend billions cracking
people's chests open,

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.320
but only rarely does it actually
prolong anyone's life.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.920
In contrast, how about wiping out
at least 90% of heart disease?

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:47.280
Think about it…

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:48.880
"Heart disease accounts&nbsp;

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.086
for more premature death"
61&nbsp;

00:08:50.086 --> 00:08:51.440
00:03:10,8 --&gt; 00:03:13,66
"than any other illness and is&nbsp;

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:52.880
almost completely preventable"

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.120
"simply by changing
diet and lifestyle."

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.680
And those same changes
can prevent or reverse

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.880
many other chronic diseases as well—
the same dietary changes.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.680
So why don't more
doctors do it?

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.840
Well, one reason is doctors
don't get paid to do it.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.640
"No one profits from
lifestyle medicine,"

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.800
"so it is not part of medical
education or practice."

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.560
Presently, physicians lack training
and financial incentives,

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.840
"So they continue to do
what they know how to do:"

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.160
"prescribe medication
and perform surgery."

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:25.520
After Dean Ornish proved
you could reverse

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:27.360
our #1 cause of death,
heart disease,

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.600
open up arteries without drugs,
without surgery,

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.280
just with a plant-based diet and
other healthy lifestyle changes,

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.760
he thought that his
studies would have

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:39.200
a "meaningful effect on the practice
of mainstream cardiology."

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:42.960
After all, a cure for
our #1 killler?!

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.520
But, he admits,
he was mistaken.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.920
Physician reimbursement,
he realized,

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.560
"is a much more powerful determinant
of medical practice than research."

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.640
Reimbursement more
than research.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.320
Salary over science.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.320
Wealth versus health.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.720
Not a very flattering portrayal
of the healing profession,

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.760
but hey, if docs won't do
it without getting paid,

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:10.880
let's get them paid.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.080
So, Dr. Ornish went
to Washington

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.360
arguing that, "Look, if we train
and pay for doctors"

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:18.800
"to learn how to help
patients address"

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.280
"the real causes of disease
with lifestyle medicine"

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.240
"and not just treat
disease risk factors"

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:25.760
"we could save trillions!"

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:28.560
And that's just talking heart disease,
diabetes, prostate, and breast cancer.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.120
The Take Back Your Health Act was
introduced in the U.S. Senate

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:36.160
"to induce doctors to learn and
practice lifestyle medicine"

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.240
not only because it works BETTER,
but here's the critical factor:

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.000
physicians will
be paid to do it.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:46.160
The bill died...

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.800
just like the millions of
Americans will continue to do

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:52.823
with reversible
chronic diseases.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.320
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:01.520
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:05.280
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:09.600
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.800
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.000
please go to the Nutrition Facts Podcast
landing page.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.520
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:24.640
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite to each of these topics.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:29.600
My latest two books are
“How to Survive a Pandemic”,

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:33.360
available on e-book, audiobook,
or actual book.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:37.440
And the “How Not to Diet Cookbook”,
with more than 100 recipes for

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.600
delicious and nutritious meals.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.960
All the proceeds I receive from
the sales of my books go to charity.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:49.600
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:51.440
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:55.600
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.440
Everything on the website is free.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.560
There’s no ads, no corporate sponsorship.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:04.080
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:07.360
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:10.320
as a tribute to my grandmother,

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:19.280
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

